@meta
id: 14-02
title: All of them, not the first one
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-basic
par: 18
minutes: 4
vscode: select the line, Ctrl-H with "in selection", Replace All
open: payout.constants.ts

@brief
The payouts importer reads `PAYOUT_BATCH` and splits it on
`PAYOUT_SEPARATOR`, which is a comma. Somebody pasted the batch in with
bars instead. Every bar on that line must become a comma.

The two type unions above and below it use bars too, and those bars are
TypeScript. They stay.

Run the substitute without any flag first and look at what happened.
Undo it. Then find the flag that means "every match on the line, not
just the first".

@keys
| `:s/|/,/` `Enter` | first bar on this line becomes a comma |
| `:s/|/,/g` `Enter` | every bar on this line |
| `u` | undo the first attempt |
| `2j` | down to the batch line |

@hints
Get the cursor onto the batch line before you type the colon. A
substitute with no range only ever touches the line you are on.
--
Without a flag, `:s` changes the first match and stops. The `g` flag
means global — but global across the line, not the file.
--
`2j` then `:s/|/,/g` and Enter. Eleven bars become eleven commas in one
command.

@solution
    2j  :s/|/,/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Chapter 11 did this exact job with `f|r,` and then `;.` eleven times.
Twenty-six keystrokes there against nine here is not the point; the
point is that nine keystrokes is the same whether the line has eleven
bars or three hundred, and you never look at any of them.

The `g` flag is the one you will forget most. Vim's default of "first
match on the line" dates from a time when a line was usually a
sentence and you usually meant one word. In code, where a line can hold
a whole array, it is almost never what you want. Most people end up
typing `g` by reflex and dropping it only when they specifically want
the first match. That is a fine habit.

One thing to notice about the pattern: `|` is a plain character here.
In the very magic dialect from chapter 13 it would mean *or*, and
`:s/\v|/,/g` would match empty strings and do something strange. Plain
magic mode, where only `\|` is alternation, is the right dialect for
this one. Choosing the dialect is part of writing the pattern.

@start payout.constants.ts
export type PayoutCurrency = 'GBP' | 'EUR' | 'USD';

export const PAYOUT_BATCH = 'PAY-2201|PAY-2202|PAY-2203|PAY-2204|PAY-2205|PAY-2206|PAY-2207|PAY-2208|PAY-2209|PAY-2210|PAY-2211|PAY-2212';
export const PAYOUT_SEPARATOR = ',';
export const PAYOUT_RETRY_LIMIT = 3;

export type PayoutState = 'queued' | 'sent' | 'settled' | 'failed';

@expected payout.constants.ts
export type PayoutCurrency = 'GBP' | 'EUR' | 'USD';

export const PAYOUT_BATCH = 'PAY-2201,PAY-2202,PAY-2203,PAY-2204,PAY-2205,PAY-2206,PAY-2207,PAY-2208,PAY-2209,PAY-2210,PAY-2211,PAY-2212';
export const PAYOUT_SEPARATOR = ',';
export const PAYOUT_RETRY_LIMIT = 3;

export type PayoutState = 'queued' | 'sent' | 'settled' | 'failed';
